<BaseLayout
  title="History"
  description="The history of the Franklin Desha House — built 1847, from the Mexican–American War through the Civil War encampment of 1863 to its National Register listing in 1986."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">The house</span>
    <h1>The History of the House</h1>
    <p>
      One of the oldest houses in Independence County, built in 1847 and lived in by the same family
      ever since.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>A timeline</h2>
    <ul class="timeline">
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1815</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          Pioneers from southwestern Kentucky begin settling the Greenbrier Bottoms and Alderbrook
          region that would become Desha, Arkansas.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1820</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          Independence County is created — Arkansas's ninth county — with Batesville as its seat, a few
          miles from where the house will stand.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1822</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          Benjamin Desha — Franklin's uncle — is appointed receiver of public moneys for the Arkansas
          Territory by President Monroe, anchoring the family in Arkansas.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1838</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          Desha County (December 12) and Searcy County (December 13) are created — named, respectively,
          for Benjamin Desha and Richard Searcy, Elizabeth Searcy's father.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1847</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          Franklin Desha — a veteran of the Mexican–American War, married to Elizabeth Searcy — builds
          this single-story double-pen dogtrot house.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1861–1865</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          The American Civil War. Franklin Desha serves in the Confederate Army.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1863</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          The property is the site of a Confederate encampment during the war.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">Later years</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          The family continues to farm the land, operating a cotton gin at Desha. The house passes down
          through the generations, always staying in the family.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">1986</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          On October 9, 1986, the Franklin Desha House is listed on the National Register of Historic
          Places (reference #86002844), recognized for its architecture and its association with the
          Desha and Searcy families.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-year">Today</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          The house remains a family home, stewarded by Robert Desha Stroud and Mary Stroud Vandiver,
          descendants of Franklin and Elizabeth Desha.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The architecture</h2>
    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        The Franklin Desha House is a <strong>single-story, double-pen dogtrot</strong> — one of the
        most distinctive and practical house forms of the nineteenth-century frontier. A dogtrot
        consists of two enclosed rooms, or "pens," separated by an open, roofed breezeway called the
        dogtrot itself. In summer the breezeway funnels air through the house; in the hard months it
        becomes covered storage, a work space, or a gathering place.
      </p>
      <p>
        The house carries a <strong>side gable roof</strong>, with a <strong>projecting gabled roof at
        the center of the main facade</strong> sheltering the front entry between the two pens. The
        form was brought west by settlers from the Upper South — including, as family records trace,
        the Kentucky line of the Desha family.
      </p>
